msgid "Configuring a Telemetry Radio using Mission Planner"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:7
#: eb8cad0421d14a37b8006d4b61f0553d
msgid "This article explains how to configure Telemetry Radios using *Mission Planner*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:12
#: bf4a74841b434c56bfe6c51c10fd0fd5
msgid "Many users will not need to configure their radios! One case where you might do so is when you use your vehicle with others — in which case you will need to specify different radio channels (Net ID)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:17
#: 1774cec62a5b4ea5a4645b6238a70d80
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:19
#: 37fd41538c9447468d4700edd1dd824c
msgid "*Mission Planner* supports configuring your radios using a simple GUI interface."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:22
#: 792aed1b119c4912a1544740217852a4
msgid "To make changes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:24
#: aabdc228855a41e98eeb939308aaf694
msgid "Connect one of the radios to your computer using the micro USB cable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:25
#: edf9d874ede940a682374223f3d4655a
msgid "Power the radio attached to the vehicle by plugging in the vehicle's battery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:27
#: 01601f681b114c47a67e23c59c98637b
msgid "Open the Mission Planner and go to the **Initial Setup \\| Optional Hardware \\| SiK Radio** page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:29
#: 033d651e94ed4b94ac25c174dfd4a066
msgid "Select the correct COM port and set the baud rate to 57600. Ensure the \"Connect\" button is in a disconnected state as shown in the image below.."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:32
#: 0582247610df4827a42fa577f2c0193e
msgid "Press the **Load Settings** button and both the *Local* and *Remote* areas should fill in with values including the firmware Version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:38
#: fd8da20ee9c141a58565f06d42b9e439
msgid "The most common entry to change is the ``Net ID``. The default is 25 for most radios but if you plan to fly in an area with other pilots who may be using the same radio it is best to change this to another number."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:42
#: a61c679992154008adc0719f3aaf1f1e
msgid "After making changes, click the **Copy Required Items to Remote** and press **Save Settings**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:45
#: 36396ee21d8445bcb72eb06fc0f5e18c
msgid "Other parameters you may choose to update:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:47
#: 96a5040603c64a90a1c2cd1f0bb06fd5
msgid "**Baud** (default 57) : the rate at which the mission planner or vehicle communicates with the local radio. \"57\" = 57600 bits per second."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:50
#: f26294ef342a437b9c271354445b4c32
msgid "**Air Speed** (default 64) : the rate at which the two radios will communicate with each other. \"64\" = 64kbps (kilobits per second). Setting a lower rate will increase the range of the radio but reduce the rate of data (i.e. the amount of data that can be sent in a given time) across the link."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:55
#: 3c9865a3a350458fa4d36490babbf1ad
msgid "**ECC** (default is \"on\"): controls whether error correction is on or off. When on \"12/24 Golay error correcting code\" is used which involves sending a 16 bit CRC byte along with the data to ensure that bad data is thrown away. Unfortunately this also halves the data rate across the link but it is recommended to leave ECC on especially when when the vehicle is far from home base because transmission errors increase greatly with distance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:62
#: bbae45503abf4b05bc573763f9270061
msgid "**MAVlink** (default is \"MAVLink\") : this controls whether the transmission is optimised for MAVLink packets or not. Set to \"Low Latency\" if using a joystick or an android tablet's virtual joystick to manually fly the vehicle. Note that information on the radio signal strength (rssi) and error rate is only sent if this parameter is set to its default, \"MAVLink\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:68
#: 74e25f751ea6499cba34b50bce4c93aa
msgid "**Tx Power** (default 20) : the transmission power where 1=1.3milliWats, 2=1.5mW, 5=3.2mW, 8=6.3mW,11=12.5mW, 14=25mW, 17=50mW, 20=100mW. This should be set to conform with your local regulations. Some per-country information :ref:`is linked here <common-telemetry-radio-regional-regulations>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:74
#: c92579270e99431fa8c36ad8611f40b2
msgid "**Duty Cycle** (default 100) : the maximum percentage of time that the radio will transmit packets. Some regions of the world allow for higher transmit power or more frequencies if you have a duty cycle below a given threshold. So for example in Europe you can transmit on a wider range of frequencies in the 433 band if your duty cycle is below 10%. Telemetry traffic is quite 'bursty', so the average transmit time is not generally high. When you set a duty cycle below 100% then your available bandwidth will be reduced, so you will find it will only work well for telemetry at higher air speeds. A radio can be set to receive only by setting its Duty Cycle to zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:84
#: 6b91ee426c414ed8a4e338020253cb55
msgid "**Max Window** (default 33) : ensure the GCS can send a packet to the vehicle ever 33msec. This should be kept as a low number (like 33) when the \"MAVLink\" setting is \"Low Latency\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:87
#: 7b37258ee2944a2f8a36eb4f537a492b
msgid "**LBT Rssi** (default 0) : holds the threshold used for \"listen before talk\" which allows it to comply with some country's regulatory requirements. When non-zero the radio listens for quiet period of time where no signals from other radios are received before transmitting. This parameter holds the receiver signal strength below which the airwaves are considered \"quiet\". If this param is set to zero then LBT is disabled. Setting this to 25 (the min) is -121 dBm. Each increment above 25 raises the threshold by about 0.5dB so for example 40 equals a signal strength of 7.5dB. The full formula is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:101
#: bcbac087b9b04bb882a042470788bee1
msgid "The LBT implementation in the radio uses a minimum listen time of 5ms, plus randomised listen time as per the European 9.2.2.2 rules. Note that in many regions you need to implement LBT in conjunction with AFA (Adaptive Frequency Agility). The radio implements AFA as long as you have ``NUM_CHANNELS`` set to more than 1."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-configuring-a-telemetry-radio-using-mission-planner.rst:106
#: 6bb0f7faea2b4f2f8541b921bbce11ec
msgid "**RTS CTS** hardware flow control. If you are using a ArduPilot firmware version released after mid 2016 and you have connected your radio to Pixhawk Telem1 Telem2 or other RTS/CTS supporting telemetry port then you can activate this, or set it to auto to improve performance."
msgstr ""
